# Spec 0033 — Materials & Library workbenches, structural samples, and serializable dispersion values

Full spec authored from the operator's preliminary spec
`specs/0027/.manual/035-materials-samples-library-preliminary-spec.md` (rev. 4,
incorporating the 036/037 dispersion refinements). The ordered work lives in
`.spec-jsonl`; this document carries only the binding constraints, the contract
table, and the per-part orientation.

## 0. Binding constraints (every part)

0.1 **The engine `…WithDisp` types stay unchanged and non-serializable.**
`EpsWithDisp` / `MuWithDisp` / `RhoWithDisp` / `OpticalPropertiesWithDisp`
(`Berreman/Berreman/Dispersion.fs:9,24,38,53`) each hold a func and MUST NOT be
altered or serialized. Every new serializable type is a `…Value` data type
(matching the engine's `EpsValue` / `MuValue` / `RhoValue` convention,
`Berreman/Berreman/MaterialProperties.fs:50,101,127`) from which a closure
BUILDS the matching func. No solver file changes in this spec.

0.2 **Elevate every primitive** (CLAUDE.md). All new ids, quantities, filters
and options MUST be named single-case DUs or records; `EpsWithDispValue`,
`MuWithDispValue` and `RhoWithDispValue` are DISTINCT types so an eps value can
never be passed where a μ or ρ value is expected; enumerated sets are DUs, not
enums; no naked `bool` in a domain record; every error case carries
`reason : string` or a typed payload.

0.3 **All catalogue IO stays mocked and in-memory.** `MaterialProxy` /
`SampleProxy` are `[<ReferenceEquality>]` records of camelCase,
`Result`-returning functions built by `createInMemory` closing over a private
mutable `Map` store — mutation confined to that closure, the one place
CLAUDE.md permits it — following the existing `Library.LibraryProxy` shape
(`Berreman/OpticalConstructor/OpticalConstructor.Domain/ElementId.fs:178-184`).
This spec writes NO catalogue files to disk, adds NO JSON schema work, and adds
NO migration or legacy-id support — every app start begins from the seeded
in-memory state. The only `OpticalConstructor.Storage` change is Part G's
import-parser breadth.

0.4 **Forbidden expansions.** There MUST be no search caching or indexing (the
linear filters stay, `…Domain/MaterialLibrary.fs:53,57`), no retries, no
configuration knobs, no versioning of the new value types, no free 3×3
gyration editor (symmetry classes only), and no clamping or validation of
dispersion segments — the FIRST covering segment wins on overlap and the
topmost segment extrapolates when no interval covers λ. New editor state MUST
be expressible and testable without a window (pure edit models plus `Handlers`
records), per CLAUDE.md's WinForms-portability rule.

0.5 **Naming.** **Materials** = materials only; **Library** = the library of
samples; the existing element-binding bay (`BayNames.library = "Library"`,
`…TestWindows/TableAndElementRotationView.fs:150`) is renamed **Selector** — a
picker, not a maintainer. `LibraryControls` / `LibraryProxy` keep their names;
a cosmetic rename is NOT in scope.

0.6 **Destructive-action UX** (operator checklist, condensed). The new
surfaces are ribbon-bay tabs (discoverability); every proxy verb is synchronous
and in-memory, so there are NO background jobs, NO progress UI and NO
pre-confirmation gate; after every verb the bay re-queries its proxy in the
same MVU render pass — no manual reload; Save/Cancel pairs sit in one row with
a visible gap and distinct positive/negative styling; Remove is confirm-gated
inline and has NO undo, justified because the store is in-memory session state
and a restart restores the seeds; no committable files are produced, so no
`.gitignore` interaction exists. (The spec-writer web pages' hidden-controls
treatment does not apply: these are desktop bays, not web pages.)

