| `<UICOMP:MaterialEditorWindow>` | component | OpticalConstructor.TestWindows | material editor window |

---

## Part A — Structural samples, elevated ids, mutating proxies (CRITICAL — land first)

### A.0 Problem statement

Today a sample's multilayer shape is faked by string-id branching:
`Propagation.sampleToSystem` matches `sample.id` against
`"sample-multilayer-qw"` / `"sample-euv-mosi"` / `"sample-active-crystal"` /
`"sample-langasite-silicon"` (`…Domain/Propagation.fs:139-141,157,172,180`)
and the material map `propertiesOf` silently falls back to transparent glass
(`Propagation.fs:104-111`). Ids are raw strings (`MaterialEntry.id`,
`…Domain/MaterialLibrary.fs:33`; `Sample.id` / `Sample.materialId`,
`…Domain/ElementId.fs:45,47`) and the only catalogue proxy is the read-only
picker `LibraryProxy` (`ElementId.fs:179`). Part A makes the stack DATA — a
`SampleStructure` with first-class, re-editable period groups — elevates ids
to `Guid`-backed DUs, and adds the mutating `MaterialProxy` / `SampleProxy`
write-seams so add / remove / edit / view / search are all unit-testable
against the stateful in-memory mock.

Recorded decisions:

- **Period groups become a first-class domain type.** This REVERSES the
  spec-010 decision that only expanded films exist (recorded in the
  `RepeatBuilder` module doc, `…Ui/RepeatBuilder.fs:1-8`): the period is now a
  live, re-editable parameter, matching FilmStar / RP-Coating practice. The
  pure expansion primitive to mirror is `RepeatBuilder.expand`
  (`…Ui/RepeatBuilder.fs:24`).
- **Material resolution is injected, never defaulted.** An unknown material id
  is a typed error surfaced once per run; the glass fallback is deleted.
- **Seeds carry fixed literal `Guid`s** so tests and the grouping trees stay
  deterministic; `removeMaterial` HARD-BLOCKS (typed `MaterialStillReferenced`)
  while any sample references the material — no cascade, no silent delete.
- Ids elevate to `MaterialId` / `SampleId` (Guid-backed single-case DUs) used
  DIRECTLY as `Map` keys in the mock stores — no wrap/unwrap layer.

## Part B — Serializable dispersion `…Value` types (engine)

### B.0 Problem statement

Catalogue materials must be serializable and editable, but the engine's
dispersion types hold funcs. Part B adds, in `Berreman/Berreman/Dispersion.fs`,
the data types that BUILD them: generic `WaveLengthInterval` / `DispersionTerm`
/ `DispersionFormula` (real by default; complex mirrors used ONLY where a model
is inherently complex, e.g. Lorentz/Drude ε); the full eps tree — isotropic /
uniaxial / biaxial, each either NON-DISPERSIVE (splitting into transparent vs
absorbing, carried by the descriptive `ConstantEpsValue` DU, never a bare
`Eps`) or DISPERSIVE (absorption inside the formulas, one shared
`wavelengthInterval` per segment) — under the two-case `EpsWithDispValue`; and
the matching two-case `RhoWithDispValue` (symmetry-class gyration,
`GyrationClass<'g>` with named multi-component records, one `GyrotropicValue<'g>`
reused by both cases) and `MuWithDispValue` (one generic Polder record
`PolderValue<'g>` reused by both cases). Assembly reuses the existing engine
constructors (`Eps.fromRefractionIndex` / `Eps.fromComplexRefractionIndex`,
`MaterialProperties.fs:76,87`; `Mu.create`, `:112`; `Rho.fromIm`, `:139`) and
the crystal-class `Rho` builders in `Berreman/OpticalProperties/Active.fs:30,38,46`
— `Active.fs` gains the missing 222 / monoclinic / triclinic builders, and
`RhoWithDispValue.toRhoWithDisp` lives there as a type extension because the
core project cannot reference `OpticalProperties`.

The editor-facing `DispersionModels.DispersionModel` catalogue
(`…Domain/DispersionModels.fs:120`) is kept and LOWERED to the new
per-axis `EpsAxisDispersion` via a new `toEpsAxis`; `ForouhiBloomer`,
`BrendelBormann` and a raw `SumOfTerms` escape hatch are added;
`AnisotropicModel` (`DispersionModels.fs:242`) is removed as superseded. The
domain edit model `MaterialComplexity` (eps ALWAYS present; magnetic / active
OPTIONAL, each itself two-case) maps to `OpticalPropertiesWithDisp` via a pure
`toProperties`. `MaterialEntry` gains `complexity : MaterialComplexity option`:
`Some` is the editable source of truth; the built-ins whose physics is
expressible as data are re-expressed, while `silicon` / `langasite` (dispersion
coded in `Berreman/OpticalProperties/Dispersive.fs:98-99`) stay preset-backed
and are view-only — their Edit affordance is REMOVED, not greyed.

## Part C — Bays: Selector rename, Materials & Library workbenches

The Main screen is a generic ribbon of bays (`Ribbon.Bay`,
`…Controls/Ribbon.fs:25`), so each change is one `BayNames` constant plus one
`mainBays` row (`…TestWindows/TableAndElementRotationView.fs:144,1577`). The
old picker bay is renamed Selector (behaviour unchanged); the new
`MaterialsControls` / `SampleLibraryControls` copy the domain-free
`LibraryControls` shape (`…Controls/LibraryControls.fs:25,34,64,76`) — pure
`Row` / `State`, a `Handlers` record of unit-returning functions, one
`[<RequireQualifiedAccess>] module UiIds` of `[<Literal>]` intent-named ids.
The host flattens proxy results into rows exactly as `libraryState` /
`flattenNode` do today (`TableAndElementRotationView.fs:1012,999`) and opens
the Part E/F editor windows; the bays go live only once those windows exist,
so no verb ever lands as a dead button.

## Part D — Shared chart control and the dual-axis n/k view

The chart model and pop-out window are REAL-MOVED (no file-linking — it mints
distinct type identities per assembly) from
`…TestWindows/ExperimentChart.fs` and `…TestWindows/ChartWindow.fs` into
`OpticalConstructor.Controls`, which gains the same `ScottPlot.Avalonia`
5.1.59 package both hosts already use (`…TestWindows/OpticalConstructor.TestWindows.fsproj:75`,
`…Ui/OpticalConstructor.Ui.fsproj:173`); the Ui-native `Charts/ChartSettings.fs`
is reconciled onto the one shared model — no third settings type. A second Y
axis (ScottPlot `plot.Axes.Right`, currently unused) is added through the
existing style spine (`ChartElement`, `ExperimentChart.fs:152`; `AxisStyle`,
`:199`; `SeriesStyle`, `:229`; `dataBounds` / `defaultState`, `:261,281`;
`rebuildPlot` / `applyAxisLimits` / `setCartesianAxesVisible`,
`ChartWindow.fs:178,133,151`). The dispersion view plots n on the LEFT axis
and k on the RIGHT, reusing the existing pure series builders
(`plotN11Series` / `plotXi11Series`, `…Ui/Charts/SeriesData.fs:103-104`,
backed by `Analytics.Variables`, `Analytics/Variables.fs:321`) and the
spectral-axis helpers (`…Ui/MaterialPreview.fs:18,31,40`), which move to the
Domain project so the TestWindows host can reach them.

## Part E — Sample editing: orientation, period groups, bulk ops

Materials are stored in PRIMARY crystallographic axes; a sample layer may sit
rotated, so each stack layer carries a serializable `CrystalOrientation` that
BUILDS a `Rotation` (`Berreman/Berreman/Geometry.fs:601,604`; conventions
`:578`; shortcuts `rotatePiX` / `rotateHalfPiY`, `:616,622`), applied at
system-build time via `Layer.rotate` (`Berreman/Berreman/Media.fs:30`) →
`OpticalProperties.rotate` (`MaterialProperties.fs:187`) — nothing is stored
rotated (worked example: `Berreman/Analytics/Examples/ActiveCrystalComparison.fsx:136`).

Recorded decision: the multi-select bulk / period operations live in a NEW
pure Domain edit model (`SampleStackEditor`) over `SampleStructure` — NOT as
`StackEditor.StackMsg` additions as the preliminary spec sketched — because
`StackEditor.applyStackMsg` transforms a resolved engine `OpticalSystem`
(`…Ui/StackEditor.fs:161,176`) that has no material ids to select by. The
existing `StackEditor` (including `groupLayers`, `StackEditor.fs:69`) is
untouched. Recorded decision: both editor windows land in
`OpticalConstructor.TestWindows` beside the existing pop-out `ChartWindow` —
the Main-screen host lives there and that project references only Domain and
Controls (`…TestWindows/OpticalConstructor.TestWindows.fsproj:79,81`), not Ui;
their edit models stay in Domain so behaviour is testable without a window.

## Part F — Material editor (progressive complexity ladder)

The simplest material is transparent, isotropic, non-dispersive; every richer
feature is unlocked by a toggle and lifts exactly one aspect of
`MaterialComplexity` off its default, and unchecking restores the default
losslessly. The anisotropy choice constrains the gyration-class picker
(isotropic ⇒ cubic classes; uniaxial ⇒ the six uniaxial enantiomorphic
classes; biaxial ⇒ orthorhombic/monoclinic/triclinic); for a non-rotating
class the activity toggle is REMOVED, not greyed. Validation reuses
`…Ui/Validation.fs` (`validateThickness:46`, `validateRepeatCount:56`,
`validateWavelengthRange:64`, `imaginaryIndexGainWarning:92`) — these helpers
move with the editors where project layering requires, never duplicated.

## Part G — RII import breadth

`MaterialImport.importRefractiveIndexInfo` (`…Storage/MaterialImport.fs:109`)
today reads only `tabulated n/nk` and `formula 1`. It is extended to RII
formulas 2–7, each lowered through a `DispersionModel` / `SumOfTerms` to an
`EpsAxisDispersion`; formulas 8 and 9 return a typed unsupported-formula
`ImportError` (`MaterialImport.fs:29`) — an honest negative scope, not a stub.

